WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.530
How does arc welding work?

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.936
On the welding table lie two steel plates.

3
00:00:06.486 --> 00:00:08.901
Arc welding melts their edges together.

4
00:00:09.451 --> 00:00:12.693
When the metal sets, they are one piece.

5
00:00:13.593 --> 00:00:15.683
The heat comes from electricity.

6
00:00:16.233 --> 00:00:22.690
The power source gives only 20 to 35 volts, but 100 to 400 amps.

7
00:00:23.241 --> 00:00:25.041
A kettle draws about 10.

8
00:00:25.941 --> 00:00:31.575
Between the wire and the steel an arc jumps across: a bright, continuous spark.

9
00:00:32.124 --> 00:00:33.924
That is where the metal melts.

10
00:00:34.824 --> 00:00:39.668
Rolls pull the wire off a spool, and push it through the hose to the torch,

11
00:00:39.868 --> 00:00:40.948
the welding gun.

12
00:00:41.848 --> 00:00:45.041
And gas flows from the bottle to the tip of the torch.

13
00:00:46.241 --> 00:00:47.843
This is MIG/MAG welding.

14
00:00:48.393 --> 00:00:53.246
The wire of 0.8 to 1.2 millimetres is the electrode,

15
00:00:53.446 --> 00:00:56.465
and melts too: that is how it fills the groove.

16
00:00:57.365 --> 00:01:00.546
Under the arc lies a glowing puddle: the weld pool.

17
00:01:01.096 --> 00:01:03.940
Behind the arc it freezes into the weld.

18
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.731
Air causes gas bubbles and a brittle weld.

19
00:01:08.281 --> 00:01:13.914
So shielding gas flows from the nozzle, usually 12 to 18 litres per minute.

20
00:01:14.464 --> 00:01:18.403
In MAG, for steel, argon with some carbon dioxide.

21
00:01:18.953 --> 00:01:21.777
In MIG, for aluminium, pure argon.

22
00:01:22.977 --> 00:01:24.811
TIG welding works differently.

23
00:01:25.361 --> 00:01:28.322
The electrode is tungsten and does not melt.

24
00:01:28.872 --> 00:01:31.925
The welder dips a separate rod into the weld pool.

25
00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:38.505
Often three to five times slower, but clean and precise: for stainless pipework,

26
00:01:38.705 --> 00:01:40.470
thin sheet and aluminium.

27
00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.990
The arc energy is the heat per millimetre of weld.

28
00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:51.662
At 24 volts, 220 amps and 400 millimetres per minute,

29
00:01:51.862 --> 00:01:55.624
that is 0.79 kilojoules per millimetre.

30
00:01:56.524 --> 00:02:01.957
In MAG you multiply by 0.8: the share that really reaches the workpiece.

31
00:02:02.507 --> 00:02:10.567
That gives a heat input of 0.63, inside the 0.5 to 0.9 of the welding procedure.

32
00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:17.077
The steel beside the weld got hot and changed: the HAZ, the heat-affected zone.

33
00:02:17.627 --> 00:02:20.553
Too much heat makes it wider and often weaker.

34
00:02:21.103 --> 00:02:24.043
With too little, the weld does not fuse.

35
00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:29.597
The wire feed speed is how many metres of wire go in each minute.

36
00:02:30.147 --> 00:02:32.272
More wire draws more current.

37
00:02:33.172 --> 00:02:37.444
On a low setting the wire keeps dipping into the pool: the short arc.

38
00:02:37.994 --> 00:02:40.200
Little heat, good for thin sheet.

39
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.318
On a high setting, with argon-rich gas,

40
00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:48.513
a fine stream of droplets flies across: the spray arc.

41
00:02:49.062 --> 00:02:52.081
Deep penetration, but only flat or horizontal.

42
00:02:52.981 --> 00:02:57.788
The deposition rate is how many kilos of weld metal are added per hour.

43
00:02:58.338 --> 00:03:04.793
Wire of 1.2 millimetres at 10 metres per minute: about 5.3 kilos an hour.

44
00:03:05.993 --> 00:03:08.930
Behind a welding robot the gas hose kinks.

45
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.998
The flow drops from 15 to 5 litres per minute.

46
00:03:13.898 --> 00:03:16.626
Within an hour the welds are full of pores.

47
00:03:17.526 --> 00:03:21.659
The fix: a sensor stops the robot below 10 litres per minute.

48
00:03:22.859 --> 00:03:25.541
A weld must also be right on paper.

49
00:03:26.091 --> 00:03:28.378
First comes the pWPS,

50
00:03:28.578 --> 00:03:31.729
the preliminary welding procedure specification:

51
00:03:31.729 --> 00:03:34.093
the recipe you test on a test piece.

52
00:03:34.993 --> 00:03:39.828
Each essential variable, such as material, gas or thickness,

53
00:03:40.028 --> 00:03:42.002
has a range of qualification.

54
00:03:42.552 --> 00:03:45.631
Step outside it, and you must test again.

55
00:03:46.531 --> 00:03:52.510
A test plate of 12 millimetres covers 3 to 24 millimetres: up to twice as thick.

56
00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.428
So 22 millimetres is covered.

57
00:03:56.328 --> 00:03:59.614
Tested at 1.2 kilojoules per millimetre.

58
00:04:00.164 --> 00:04:05.795
With impact requirements you may go up to 25 percent higher: up to 1.5.

59
00:04:06.344 --> 00:04:08.538
So 1.6 is outside.

60
00:04:09.438 --> 00:04:14.198
The welder holds a certificate to ISO 9606-1.

61
00:04:14.748 --> 00:04:17.828
The welding coordinator oversees which welder

62
00:04:17.828 --> 00:04:20.565
and which procedure belong to each weld.

63
00:04:21.765 --> 00:04:23.239
The weld cools down.

64
00:04:23.789 --> 00:04:29.002
t8/5 is the time in seconds from 800 to 500 degrees.

65
00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:34.943
If it is too short, martensite can form in the HAZ: hard,

66
00:04:35.143 --> 00:04:37.384
brittle steel that cracks easily.

67
00:04:38.284 --> 00:04:41.941
The carbon equivalent says how readily a steel hardens.

68
00:04:42.491 --> 00:04:49.495
A CEV of 0.46 is high, and plate of 40 millimetres draws the heat away fast.

69
00:04:50.695 --> 00:04:53.609
So preheat, so the weld cools more slowly.

70
00:04:54.159 --> 00:04:57.607
And measure the interpass temperature before every pass.

71
00:04:58.507 --> 00:05:01.317
A hydrogen crack can appear days later.

72
00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:05.431
Inspection often waits 16 to 48 hours.

73
00:05:06.331 --> 00:05:11.744
On stainless steel the heat tint beside the weld must come off, or it rusts there.
